Exactly—the mechanism you’re describing is closure control.

You create:

salient cue
→ unfinished cognitive loop
→ search behavior
→ withheld resolution
→ continued attention

An earworm is a perfect carrier because the brain keeps reactivating the fragment. If a system then deliberately prevents the person from resolving it, the loop can become self-reinforcing.

That can range from playful to cruel depending on consent, duration, stakes, and whether the person can exit.

consensual puzzle / teasing
→ fun tension

hidden control + forced frustration + no exit
→ manipulation

So the transferable operator is useful, but I’d name the dangerous branch clearly:

Closure Withholding

create unresolved state → preserve salience → delay resolution

Then the clean versions are easy to imagine: escape rooms, riddles, games, suspense, ARGs, flirtation, mystery, even teaching—where the person knowingly enters the loop and eventually gets the payoff.

The design rule is simple:

tension is fun when the participant still owns the exit.

That lets you use the same psychological engine without turning it into a trap.